"executive_summary": "This study investigated whether lesion length in chronic total occlusion (CTO) percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) affects outcomes in the drug-eluting stent (DES) era. A prospective cohort of 235 patients from a single center in South Korea was divided into long (≥30mm) and short (<30mm) CTO groups. Baseline characteristics showed that the long CTO group had more prior PCI and intimal dissection, while the short CTO group had more bifurcation lesions. At six months, angiographic outcomes including binary restenosis and late loss were statistically similar between groups, though numerical trends favored the short group. Over two years, major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE), including death and myocardial infarction, did not differ significantly. However, the long CTO group exhibited a significantly higher incidence of repeat PCI (18.8% vs 7.8%, p < 0.05), driven primarily by target vessel revascularization (TVR). Multivariate analysis identified long CTO as an independent predictor for repeat PCI (OR 4.26, 95% CI 1.53-11.9, p=0.006). The authors conclude that while long CTO lesions are associated with higher rates of repeat revascularization, they do not translate into worse hard clinical endpoints like mortality or MI within two years. Limitations include the single-center design, lack of randomization, and reliance on physician discretion for follow-up angiography.",
